What is OLEDoS?
OLEDoS (OLED on Silicon) is a self‑emissive micro‑display architecture in which organic light‑emitting polymers are deposited directly onto a single‑crystal silicon CMOS wafer that simultaneously serves as the active backplane. This integration enables pixel‑by‑pixel driving, exceptionally high pixel densities, and the ability to tailor drive circuitry for power‑efficient operation. A 1.3‑inch OLEDoS module typically measures an effective diagonal of 1.30–1.35 inches, delivering resolutions that range from 2K (≈2,000 × 1,300 pixels) to 4K (≈3,840 × 2,560 pixels) within a footprint that can be accommodated by the most slender head‑mounted devices on the market.
